{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "issue_category",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.95,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Categorising incidents by issue type gives a strong grouping axis to find which categories are resolved inefficiently. Values map soundly from short_description (printer->printer, connectivity->network)."
    },
    {
      "name": "hardware_failure_pattern",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.55,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "A useful failure-mode axis for driving root-cause analysis, with sensible NULL for silent rows. However 'mechanical_jam' is asserted from generic text like 'Printer not working properly' and 'The office printer is not functioning', which do not indicate a jam. That confident specific where the text is silent is a hallucination that weakens groundedness."
    },
    {
      "name": "assigned_technician",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.3,
      "source_groundedness": 0.35,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Technician identity could matter for resolution efficiency, but the table already has 'assigned_to'/'closed_by', making this largely redundant. More critically, the sample source text only exposes short_description/timestamps; the specific person names appear invented for the given rows rather than clearly derived, and identical descriptions get different technicians without visible source support."
    },
    {
      "name": "issue_specificity",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Whether a description is generic vs specific is directly relevant to inefficient resolution (vague tickets take longer). Values are a sound reading of the text: 'not working properly'->generic_malfunction, 'on floor 3'->location_context, 'connect to the database'->specific_symptom."
    },
    {
      "name": "repeated_issue_pattern",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.6,
      "source_groundedness": 0.4,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "A recurring-issue flag is a genuinely useful driver axis. But per-row 'true'/'false' is not supportable from a single row's source text alone; it requires cross-row comparison, and the near-50/50 split with no visible evidence in samples suggests the flag is not clearly grounded for individual rows."
    },
    {
      "name": "caller_type",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.4,
      "source_groundedness": 0.45,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Distinguishing itil_user vs named_employee could offer a minor grouping axis, but is largely derivable from the existing caller_id and adds limited leverage for THIS efficiency query. The sample source text shown does not include caller_id, so the assigned values (e.g. itil_user for the connectivity row) are not visibly grounded and appear partly asserted."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.7,
  "source_groundedness": 0.6,
  "rationale": "The set provides several strong new axes for diagnosing inefficient resolution: issue_category and issue_specificity are both useful and well grounded in the short_description text. hardware_failure_pattern adds a good failure-mode dimension but over-specifies failure causes (e.g. mechanical_jam) from generic text. assigned_technician and caller_type are partly redundant with existing columns and their per-row values are not visibly derivable from the shown source text. repeated_issue_pattern is analytically relevant but a per-row boolean is hard to justify from single-row evidence. Overall useful, with moderate groundedness concerns from confident specifics unsupported by the text."
}
